#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125299} :activity/id #uuid "8cf4b4e2-a2a4-5056-88ef-3cbe1bd9eadd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050555 :topic/id #uuid "52ee4eec-32eb-50f8-908d-64e67d09f541" :topic/math-academy-id 1938} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125736 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and a unit vector $\\mathbf{u} = \\left\\langle  u_1, u_2 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nThe **directional derivative of $f$** at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}$ is\n\n$$\nf'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0) = \\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0}\\dfrac{f(\\mathbf x_0 + t \\mathbf u) - f(\\mathbf x_0)}{t}.\n$$\n\nThe directional derivative of $f$ is the rate of change of the function $f$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}.$ We'll discuss this idea more later in the lesson.\n\nIt turns out that if $f$ is differentiable, we can write $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ in terms of partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0) = \\nabla f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\mathbf u.\n$$\n\nAt the end of the lesson, we'll derive this formula. But for now, let's get some practice using it.\n\nFor example, let's find the directional derivative of the function $f(x, y) = x^2 + y^2$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)=(1,2)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2) & =(2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(2\\cdot 2)\\,j \\\\  & =2i+4j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,2) & =∇\\,f(1,2)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(2\\,i+4\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "925cfc95-04b9-5e98-bec4-99d0e4ae3a54" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2503 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7b456d3e-c653-5713-8112-18d37fb77751" :step/math-academy-id 11245 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125300}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :question/id #uuid "bf35c502-5a22-5953-9158-b2464d28c09f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3377"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518124}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "e45885a4-1bb2-5e1b-b77d-8445bc691679" :step/math-academy-id 11246 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125737 :tutorial/content "From the formula for the directional derivative, we get that if  $z = f (x, y)$ is a differentiable function and $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector, then\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'_{\\mathbf u} &= \\nabla f \\cdot \\mathbf u \\[3pt]\n                      &= |\\nabla f| |\\mathbf u| \\cos\\theta\\[3pt]\n                      & = |\\nabla f| \\cos\\theta,\n}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the vectors $\\nabla f$ and $\\mathbf u,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/50/50c82908d5fd619c1c3c45f2aa3ff26929b8f369233c08d0688a091d2d3c123c.png)\n\n \n\nIt follows that $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is maximized when $\\theta =0.$ In other words, $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is maximized when $\\mathbf u$ and $\\nabla f$ point in the same direction. Thus, the maximum value of $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is $|\\nabla f|.$\n\nA similar argument shows that $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ reaches its minimum value $- |\\nabla f|$ when $\\theta = \\pi;$ that is, when $\\nabla f$ and $\\mathbf u$ point in opposite directions.\n\nAs a result, we get the following theorem:\n\n*A differentiable function $f$ increases most rapidly in the direction of the gradient and decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction to that of its gradient.*\n\nFor functions of more than two variables, the same theorem holds." :tutorial/id #uuid "5e4c2cac-e80c-51dd-8138-681fa98ae521" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2505 :tutorial/title "The Gradient as the Direction of Maximum Increase of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "59fc7193-5cff-5a04-9436-423d956e8140" :step/math-academy-id 11253 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518218}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "5e11bfb6-5859-570a-a461-b440db1620c4" :step/math-academy-id 11257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :question/id #uuid "1eea4610-508a-5437-802e-9d009b777e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-3382"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501201}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"} :step/id #uuid "67953a72-0311-5e24-ad89-dce2e8e4793b" :step/math-academy-id 11260 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125305}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125738 :tutorial/content "For a surface $z = f(x,y),$ the partial derivative $f_x(x_0,y_0)$ gives us the rate of change of $f$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{i} = \\langle 1,0\\rangle.$ But this is just a special case of the directional derivative, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{i}^{′}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & ={f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{j}^{′}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & ={f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the directional derivative generalizes the notion of a partial derivative. \n\nNow, recall that the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ represent the rate of change of $f$ in the direction of vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j,$ respectively. As for a general directional derivative, we obtain the following property:\n\n*The directional derivative $f'_\\mathbf{u}(x_0,y_0)$ represents the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "40fa091e-c99f-5d19-8681-e9656e23dc2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2508 :tutorial/title "The Directional Derivative as a Generalization of the Partial Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "d49395bc-14f6-5290-8963-de30d5d2fe22" :step/math-academy-id 11285 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125739 :tutorial/content "Consider a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ and suppose that the point $P$ with coordinates $(x_0,y_0, f(x_0,y_0))$ lies on $S.$ We wish to derive a formula for the directional derivative $f'_\\mathbf{u}(x_0,y_0),$ where $\\mathbf u = \\langle u_1, u_2 \\rangle$ is a unit vector.\n\nGeometrically, $f'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0)$ can be constructed in the following way:\n\n- Define a vertical plane $\\Pi$ that's parallel to $\\mathbf u$ and passes through $P.$\n\n- Define the curve $C$ as the intersection of the surface $S$ and the plane $\\Pi.$\n\n- Then, the directional derivative $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$\n\nThe geometric interpretation of $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is similar to that of the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y.$ The only difference here is that we are not restricting the plane $\\Pi$ to be parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nNow, since $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is tangent to $C$ and is in the direction of $\\mathbf u$ at $P,$ the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf v = \\langle u_1, u_2, f'_{\\mathbf u}\\rangle\n$$\n\nis tangent to our surface $S$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/59/59cb8fa410b9194c298d8f477ab0d0cff3e27765bc45c14cb9b5ea9210288184.png)\n\nRecall that $\\langle f_x (x_0,y_0), f_y(x_0,y_0), -1\\rangle$ is known to be a normal vector to the surface at $P.$ Since this vector must be perpendicular to $\\mathbf v,$ the dot product of the two vectors must be zero, which means that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\langle u_1, u_2, f'_{\\mathbf u}\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_x (x_0,y_0), f_y(x_0,y_0), -1\\rangle &=0 \\[5pt]\nu_1 f_x (x_0,y_0) + u_2 f_y (x_0,y_0) - f'_{\\mathbf u} &=0 \\[5pt]\nf'_{\\mathbf u} = \\nabla f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\mathbf u. }\n$$\n\nWe can interpret this formula as the projection of $\\nabla f (x_0,y_0)$ onto the direction of $\\mathbf u,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/82/82e7dece2597e15172600ff598090241facb834a7248ed54286fa777c25a6ebd.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "baa62c26-a25a-5293-a28f-128d45e61d68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2509 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula For the Directional Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "61d92228-7cc0-5d7f-93a0-5595777edad8" :step/math-academy-id 11286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125825 :tutorial/content "What is the directional derivative of the function $\\mathbf{f}$ at $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of the gradient? \n\nLet's apply our formula. First, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\nabla\\! f\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0}){‖}^{2}}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude the following:\n\n*The directional derivative in the direction of the gradient equals the norm of the gradient. Furthermore, the norm of the gradient can be interpreted as the rate of change of the function in the direction of the gradient.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "f8d8fbab-9897-5441-9c15-b0bc1455ed67" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4469 :tutorial/title "The Rate of Change in the Direction of the Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "c95e7b32-1764-5c4a-a384-792d274c2f3a" :step/math-academy-id 27522 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125303}}] :activity/title "Directional Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124730}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501275} :activity/id #uuid "0a41176c-b39d-554a-935e-72997f04c98e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050555 :topic/id #uuid "52ee4eec-32eb-50f8-908d-64e67d09f541" :topic/math-academy-id 1938} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501247 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f0ad0a-171c-5322-8cf2-cfca08972fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500510} {:answer/id #uuid "407711da-42ac-56dc-a3f6-af974631b602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500594} {:answer/id #uuid "5490207d-8efb-5f97-80cd-1bd0f27685fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} {:answer/id #uuid "86e21ca8-366b-5985-a073-18a5cd00683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500749} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4c7ab4-1278-5730-804a-3c97ca86d365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcae1503-ee5b-5bcc-859b-64edce5a1f65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a3fef551-e9bd-56f6-a5fb-6131de39b9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86542" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨2,4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}y},\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}(y-1)}{x{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 1},\\,\\frac{{e}^{1}\\cdot (1-1)}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-e\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a60e94a-7f5d-54b3-9400-bd9ba416bdb2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8837b541-d3d8-5ebe-8ea8-af61591cb6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322232} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f03f12-e1ac-5472-a11e-f1f87bcd9621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322572} {:answer/id #uuid "c1717e43-53c5-54f5-8c5f-68df4b9cfd08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322650} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a54f97-4039-5587-a94f-458a55a1776d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ef68e3-e12b-57d6-890b-83987a1922bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a40605a-36ce-555d-abab-e5a81e672aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3a0c033d-5079-51f1-a44e-c13c59694293" :question/math-academy-id "q-37083" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7cf532da-0f94-51c8-af70-3482c03bc391" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172f3f98-43e9-586f-8bdf-a9ddf4ae061e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,i-12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500519} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5dcfde-da03-5e9b-8257-b3a15610fd0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,i+18\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500690} {:answer/id #uuid "9305e79c-d3f6-5049-b273-0bc89befd2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\,i+4\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500772} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2acabe-a8aa-5bd5-a28c-992a7b2ef314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500782} {:answer/id #uuid "e95111ed-e4a8-5a81-b040-27eaaa405bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+9\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500690} :answer-field/id #uuid "35227601-10b3-594c-aac4-a002a6f61ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c3c8fd2-c84f-5ffa-82e1-4f53ac4509f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-130277" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=2(xy+1)^{3/2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,j \\\\  & =3y\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,i+3x\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =3(1)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,i+3(3)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+18\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $6\\,i+18\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "892b58dc-320d-53ce-b8f0-06a4365c54d1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fb6012-956b-5c39-8935-74645f89ee82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i+2\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500483} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0516fc-fa33-5a32-bc85-021583caaccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500790} {:answer/id #uuid "a91d4692-51f1-5ef6-96a3-68a85bcad337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500817} {:answer/id #uuid "b20c5f55-306b-5cef-9a67-804b11be245c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c25011-b246-57b2-afb4-9949bd083e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i+\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} :answer-field/id #uuid "941d1058-20a9-5c97-a5b7-7c5da913add0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2926681-2f02-501e-9c4c-db877ffeff07" :question/math-academy-id "q-83562" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\pi ,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2xy)\\text{ }i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(\\pi ,0) & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot 0)\\text{ }i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{\\pi }^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "d394d923-4ab3-59e3-94fd-7981e3aa060d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501247}}] :activity/title "Directional Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500458}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504723} :activity/id #uuid "e2086f71-2e1e-548f-97d0-b3f66004887f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050555 :topic/id #uuid "52ee4eec-32eb-50f8-908d-64e67d09f541" :topic/math-academy-id 1938} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504052} {:answer/id #uuid "169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504072} {:answer/id #uuid "24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504094} {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} {:answer/id #uuid "2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130279" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "1a187383-65b5-5345-b337-dc86407bb551" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504734}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f931965-4230-53c9-9c4e-51f593f9d015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321267} {:answer/id #uuid "57f67efd-2b45-5e67-a5e4-4b0fd709ba1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321837} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1a8d46-fbc3-5f92-8b69-6709f7023c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322114} {:answer/id #uuid "cda4641b-3880-5a2f-a60a-2d4b84c1090a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322750} {:answer/id #uuid "d4216551-4828-50f1-870a-15d1ba740ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{82}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322802} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e6c5b32-a96f-564b-b174-aa4099e65f9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a13cba05-5f4a-5d75-8f65-f72eb54e0d73" :question/math-academy-id "q-36300" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2xy+{e}^{3x-y}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(2y+3{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-(2x-{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(1,3) & =-(2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }i-(2\\cdot 1-{e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction of $-∇f(1,3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(1,3)‖ & =‖-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{82}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4765b3cd-4d39-5e89-aa2a-00187802b850" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504731}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504731 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ff85bb-8ce2-5025-a0a1-83fc98417ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2\\pi ,-2,-\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321217} {:answer/id #uuid "636513d9-9ad2-5bbe-b8b3-cd67a4bf8d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321920} {:answer/id #uuid "9f98fa5a-9408-52f3-8b7e-8f25e3ab2ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi ,-1,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322407} {:answer/id #uuid "c672059e-d68f-556c-9a70-117d11270e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322686} {:answer/id #uuid "cdeb1fca-19f0-5446-8266-9a4f0c5b2778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322752}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322752} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3b7500-df79-56f9-843a-ede4ff70a4be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a412c3a-6b92-5f8b-8cf9-ec76f9ca97fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-36305" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z),\\,2y,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1,2) & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2),\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi ),\\,2,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "99beae54-c55d-54a9-84c9-5b047a305e17" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a5cc75b-1aa1-5b30-9362-5f8752961da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263250} {:answer/id #uuid "1d98f894-779c-5822-84b3-85b9a53ad6b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263271} {:answer/id #uuid "4efb98a8-8a82-5879-9aee-9a09d6afe439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263687} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb30fc7-6c64-5f56-9344-f70ad5eab684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264636} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a55fec-f5cc-59e3-8bae-75fcaf8ea6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265001} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ef6581-d8af-5b79-a84d-4f8dc281dbef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "129e769a-9e81-5bb8-a357-71dd7d678811" :question/math-academy-id "q-130801" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1}-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}},\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{(-1)}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0(1)+0(-\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0270345-59d4-5ead-8ae3-ee6cac39422a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371188b1-b7ec-57b7-b295-164f4505e38f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263494} {:answer/id #uuid "468d5107-6a48-54fc-a274-623f490c7ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263617} {:answer/id #uuid "5224940d-41f5-5791-9620-631ff60b6036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,i+10\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263707} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9a260e-6d34-52c4-a50b-774f0edf8d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263781} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f338bb-07ad-5dd5-a554-45630ad32b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263707} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b1bccb-f245-5952-bbe0-74fd47a6963c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "234c72df-7b34-5698-b613-f1acebf8fd8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130276" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+3y)\\,i+(3x+4y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(2(2)+3(1))\\,i+(3(2)+4(1))\\,j \\\\  & =7\\,i+10\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $7\\,i+10\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "ff89b137-8638-5945-ad2a-c71960c68d36" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504714}}] :activity/title "Directional Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504029}]}
